Subject: [PATCH] imx296: external hardware trigger (Fast Trigger mode) for the J106 rig

The four IMX296 on J106 ports C-F each free-run on their own on-module
oscillator, so their frame clocks drift against each other without bound -
exactly the failure a global shutter is supposed to remove. The sensor can
instead take its frame timing from its XTRIG pin, which is the only way to make
four independent modules expose at the same instant.

This adds that mode. The trigger itself is generated off-board by a hardware
timer (see hw-trigger/WIRING.md for why not from the TX2: no PWM-capable pad on
this carrier is free, so a TX2-sourced trigger would be a software-toggled GPIO,
and in this mode timing jitter turns directly into exposure noise).

Fast Trigger mode is used, not Sequential Trigger:

  - Fast Trigger is a MASTER-mode feature (XMASTER low), which is how these
    modules are already strapped - proven by the fact that they free-run at all.
    No strapping change, and no XVS/XHS wiring: the sensor keeps its own
    timebase and only the frame start comes from outside. One wire, not three.
  - Sequential Trigger is slave-mode only and would additionally require an
    externally generated 67.5 kHz XHS fanned out to four boards.

Exposure becomes the XTRIG low pulse width (t_exp = t_low + 14.26 us), so it is
no longer a sensor register. Three consequences, all handled here:

  - set_exposure() stops writing SHS1 while triggered. It still returns success,
    because failing it would fail the stream, but it says once per stream that
    the value is being ignored and where the exposure actually comes from.

  - set_frame_rate() pins VMAX to the readout mode's own length while triggered.
    Frame period comes from XTRIG, so VMAX only has to describe the readout -
    and leaving it under control of the frame_rate control is actively harmful:
    with override_enable=1 tegracam re-applies that control at its DT *minimum*
    of 2 fps, VMAX becomes 33750, and VI then dies with PXL_SOF syncpt timeout
    for the rest of the boot. This is the trap already documented in README
    Stage 7; in trigger mode it is now structurally impossible.

  - The selection is latched in set_mode(), not start_streaming(), because
    tegracam runs set_mode() -> control overrides -> start_streaming() and the
    two handlers above must already know the mode when the overrides land.

The mode change itself has to ride on the standby cycle that stream start
already performs - the sensor forbids changing fast-trigger mode during
operation - so TRIGEN/LOWLAGTRG/SYNCSEL are written after STANDBY is cleared but
before XMSTA is released. That ordering, and the SYNCSEL byte values, follow the
Raspberry Pi imx296 driver (rpi-6.6.y), the reference implementation for this
mode. SYNCSEL is written as a whole byte (0xc0 normal / 0xf0 Hi-Z) rather than
as its documented [5:4] field, because bits [7:6] must stay 11b - something only
that driver records, not the datasheet.

Control is a module parameter rather than a device tree property. All four
sensors are driven from ONE shared XTRIG net, so there is no per-sensor
configuration to express; and this way switching modes needs no DTB rebuild and
no reboot, which makes the free-running/triggered comparison - the acceptance
test for the wiring - a two-second operation. Name and semantics match the
Raspberry Pi driver's: trigger_mode, 0 = default, 1 = XTRIG.

Default is 0, so a board with no trigger wired behaves exactly as before.

  echo 1 | sudo tee /sys/module/imx296/parameters/trigger_mode

Applies on top of 0002. No device tree change.
--- a/kernel/nvidia/drivers/media/i2c/imx296.c
+++ b/kernel/nvidia/drivers/media/i2c/imx296.c
@@ -42,13 +42,43 @@
 
 #include "imx296_mode_tbls.h"
 
+/*
+ * External hardware trigger (IMX296 "Fast Trigger" mode).
+ *
+ * 0 = free-running (default, and what the sensor has always done here)
+ * 1 = the sensor waits for a falling edge on its XTRIG pin, and the LOW pulse
+ *     width sets the exposure - see hw-trigger/WIRING.md.
+ *
+ * Deliberately a module parameter and not a device-tree property: the J106
+ * rig drives all four IMX296 from ONE shared XTRIG net, so there is no
+ * per-sensor configuration to express, and this way switching modes needs no
+ * DTB rebuild and no reboot. Read at set_mode(), so it takes effect on the
+ * next stream start. Name and semantics match the Raspberry Pi imx296 driver.
+ */
+static int trigger_mode;
+module_param(trigger_mode, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(trigger_mode,
+	"0 = free-running (default), 1 = external XTRIG hardware trigger");
+
 /* Register addresses (16-bit address, 8-bit data).
  * Registers wider than 8 bits are little-endian across consecutive
  * addresses - see imx296_read_mbreg()/imx296_write_mbreg(). */
 #define IMX296_CTRL00				0x3000
 #define IMX296_CTRL00_STANDBY			BIT(0)
+#define IMX296_CTRL0A				0x300a
+#define IMX296_CTRL0A_XMSTA			BIT(0)
+#define IMX296_CTRL0B				0x300b
+#define IMX296_CTRL0B_TRIGEN			BIT(0)
 #define IMX296_CTRL08				0x3008
 #define IMX296_CTRL08_REGHOLD			BIT(0)
+/* XVS/XHS pin direction. These are whole-byte values, NOT a [5:4] bitfield:
+ * bits [7:6] must stay 11b. Taken from the Raspberry Pi imx296 driver, which
+ * is the only source that documents the reserved bits. */
+#define IMX296_SYNCSEL				0x3036
+#define IMX296_SYNCSEL_NORMAL			0xc0
+#define IMX296_SYNCSEL_HIZ			0xf0
+#define IMX296_LOWLAGTRG			0x30ae
+#define IMX296_LOWLAGTRG_FAST			BIT(0)
 #define IMX296_VMAX				0x3010	/* 24-bit, VMAX[19:0] */
 #define IMX296_HMAX				0x3014	/* 16-bit */
 #define IMX296_INCKSEL(n)			(0x3089 + (n))
@@ -131,6 +161,8 @@
 	u32				frame_length;	/* VMAX, lines */
 	u32				hmax;
 	bool				mono;
+	bool				triggered;	/* latched from trigger_mode */
+	bool				trig_exp_warned;
 };
 
 static const struct regmap_config sensor_regmap_config = {
@@ -265,6 +297,28 @@
 	if (val <= 0)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
+	/*
+	 * Under an external trigger the frame period is set by XTRIG, not by
+	 * VMAX, so VMAX must simply describe the readout. Pin it to the mode's
+	 * own length: otherwise tegracam's override pass re-applies the
+	 * frame_rate control at its DT *minimum* (2 fps), VMAX becomes 33750,
+	 * and VI dies with PXL_SOF syncpt timeout for the rest of the boot.
+	 */
+	if (priv->triggered) {
+		frame_length = s_data->fmt_height + IMX296_MIN_VBLANK;
+
+		dev_dbg(dev, "%s: triggered; VMAX pinned to %u [lines]\n",
+			__func__, frame_length);
+
+		err = imx296_write_mbreg(priv, IMX296_VMAX, frame_length, 3);
+		if (err)
+			return err;
+
+		priv->frame_length = frame_length;
+
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	frame_length = (u32)div64_u64(
 		IMX296_INTERNAL_CLK_HZ *
 			(u64)mode->control_properties.framerate_factor,
@@ -311,6 +365,21 @@
 	if (val < 0)
 		val = 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * In trigger mode the exposure is the XTRIG low pulse width, generated
+	 * off-board; SHS1 does not control it. Accept the call so the stream
+	 * does not fail, but do not pretend to program the sensor.
+	 */
+	if (priv->triggered) {
+		if (!priv->trig_exp_warned) {
+			dev_info(dev,
+				"exposure comes from the XTRIG pulse width in external-trigger mode; ignoring %lld (set it on the trigger generator)\n",
+				val);
+			priv->trig_exp_warned = true;
+		}
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	/* exposure time -> internal-clock ticks */
 	ticks = div64_u64((u64)val * IMX296_INTERNAL_CLK_HZ,
 		(u64)mode->control_properties.exposure_factor);
@@ -617,6 +686,15 @@
 	unsigned int i;
 	int err;
 
+	/*
+	 * Latch the trigger selection here, not in start_streaming(): tegracam
+	 * runs set_mode() -> ctrl overrides -> start_streaming(), so the
+	 * exposure and frame_rate handlers below already need to know which
+	 * mode this stream is in by the time the overrides are applied.
+	 */
+	priv->triggered = (trigger_mode == 1);
+	priv->trig_exp_warned = false;
+
 	err = imx296_write_table(priv, mode_table[IMX296_MODE_COMMON]);
 	if (err)
 		return err;
@@ -651,11 +729,60 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Start of stream, with the trigger-mode selection folded in.
+ *
+ * The sensor forbids changing fast-trigger mode during operation, so the mode
+ * change has to ride on the standby cycle the stream start already performs:
+ * leave standby, program TRIGEN/LOWLAGTRG/SYNCSEL while master operation is
+ * still stopped, and only then release XMSTA. This is the same ordering the
+ * Raspberry Pi imx296 driver uses, which is the reference implementation for
+ * this mode.
+ *
+ * Replaces the old imx296_start_stream[] table, which could not express
+ * "write something in the middle".
+ */
 static int imx296_start_streaming(struct tegracam_device *tc_dev)
 {
 	struct imx296 *priv = (struct imx296 *)tegracam_get_privdata(tc_dev);
+	struct camera_common_data *s_data = tc_dev->s_data;
+	struct device *dev = tc_dev->dev;
+	bool trig = priv->triggered;
+	int err;
+
+	/* Leave software standby. */
+	err = imx296_write_reg(s_data, IMX296_CTRL00, 0);
+	if (err)
+		return err;
+
+	usleep_range(5000, 6000);
+
+	err = imx296_write_reg(s_data, IMX296_CTRL0B,
+		trig ? IMX296_CTRL0B_TRIGEN : 0);
+	if (err)
+		return err;
+
+	err = imx296_write_reg(s_data, IMX296_LOWLAGTRG,
+		trig ? IMX296_LOWLAGTRG_FAST : 0);
+	if (err)
+		return err;
+
+	/*
+	 * XVS/XHS are outputs in master mode. Park them high-Z while triggered
+	 * so that several modules wired in parallel cannot contend on those
+	 * pads; restore the normal setting when free-running.
+	 */
+	err = imx296_write_reg(s_data, IMX296_SYNCSEL,
+		trig ? IMX296_SYNCSEL_HIZ : IMX296_SYNCSEL_NORMAL);
+	if (err)
+		return err;
+
+	dev_info(dev, "streaming: %s\n", trig ?
+		"EXTERNAL TRIGGER (XTRIG, fast trigger; exposure = pulse width)" :
+		"free-running");
 
-	return imx296_write_table(priv, mode_table[IMX296_START_STREAM]);
+	/* Release master stop - from here the sensor runs, or waits for XTRIG. */
+	return imx296_write_reg(s_data, IMX296_CTRL0A, 0);
 }
 
 static int imx296_stop_streaming(struct tegracam_device *tc_dev)
--- a/kernel/nvidia/drivers/media/i2c/imx296_mode_tbls.h
+++ b/kernel/nvidia/drivers/media/i2c/imx296_mode_tbls.h
@@ -31,12 +31,9 @@
  * Datasheet: CTRL00[0] = STANDBY, CTRL0A[0] = XMSTA (master start/stop).
  * Order matters - leave standby first, let the internal PLL settle, then
  * release XMSTA. Stopping is the exact reverse. */
-static imx296_reg imx296_start_stream[] = {
-	{0x3000, 0x00},			/* CTRL00: clear STANDBY */
-	{IMX296_TABLE_WAIT_MS, 5},
-	{0x300a, 0x00},			/* CTRL0A: release XMSTA */
-	{IMX296_TABLE_END, 0x00}
-};
+/* There is no start-stream table: the start sequence has to write the
+ * trigger-mode registers between clearing STANDBY and releasing XMSTA, which
+ * a flat table cannot express. See imx296_start_streaming(). */
 
 static imx296_reg imx296_stop_stream[] = {
 	{0x300a, 0x01},			/* CTRL0A: XMSTA (master stop) */
@@ -114,14 +111,12 @@
 enum {
 	IMX296_MODE_1456x1088_60FPS,
 	IMX296_MODE_COMMON,
-	IMX296_START_STREAM,
 	IMX296_STOP_STREAM,
 };
 
 static imx296_reg *mode_table[] = {
 	[IMX296_MODE_1456x1088_60FPS]	= imx296_mode_1456x1088,
 	[IMX296_MODE_COMMON]		= imx296_mode_common,
-	[IMX296_START_STREAM]		= imx296_start_stream,
 	[IMX296_STOP_STREAM]		= imx296_stop_stream,
 };
 
